{"type": "result", "subtype": "success", "is_error": false, "num_turns": 6, "result": "I checked whether BillingService exposes an API of its own that other services call. It doesn't, so here's the result.\n\n**Answer: if BillingService goes down, 2 services stop working within two hops: RenewalsWorker and ReportingService.**\n\n**Hop 1 (services that depend on BillingService or call its API):**\n- **RenewalsWorker**: RenewalsWorker DEPENDS_ON BillingService\n- **ReportingService**: ReportingService DEPENDS_ON BillingService\n\n**Hop 2 (services that depend on those):** none. A depth-2 search found nothing that depends on or calls RenewalsWorker or ReportingService.\n\n**Why the count isn't higher:**\n- **BillingService exposes no API in the graph.** There's no \"BillingAPI\", so nothing reaches BillingService through an API call. The only links in are the two DEPENDS_ON edges above.\n- **PaymentsAPI belongs to PaymentsService, not BillingService.** It came up as a similar name, and OrderService and RenewalsWorker call it. But a PaymentsService outage is a separate question, so OrderService, SearchService and ApiGateway aren't affected by BillingService going down.\n- **Things BillingService uses don't count.** It stores data in BillingPostgresDatabase, calls NotificationsAPI (exposed by NotificationsService) and depends on OrderService. Those are what it needs to run. They don't stop working when it goes down.\n\nNo configuration keys are involved.", "usage": {"input_tokens": 12, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 4276, "cache_read_input_tokens": 26578, "output_tokens": 1265, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 324}, "server_tool_use": {"web_search_requests": 0, "web_fetch_requests": 0}, "service_tier": "standard", "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 4276, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "inference_geo": "global", "iterations": [{"input_tokens": 2, "output_tokens": 579, "cache_read_input_tokens": 5578, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 356, "cache_cr